Subject: Cantor Trusted Compilation Profile

Description: A fail-closed trust boundary requiring Cantor to consume only cryptographically verified compiled SOP packages and forbidding unsigned material from acquiring semantic or executable authority.

& [ProfileIdentity] is the canonical SJS security profile
  + [specification_uuid] is 12038e12-3dcb-4a3f-991a-cd54d3046118
  + [status] is mandatory_Cantor_authority_gate
  + [purpose] is bind every Cantor-authorized meaning and instruction to recognized source authority and a verified compilation artifact

& [CRCTerm] is the protected terminology
  + [canonical_expansion] is Cantor Recognition Certificate
  + [meaning] is a cryptographically signed package manifest recognized by Cantor trust policy
  + [excluded_meaning] is cyclic redundancy check
  = correction: a conventional CRC detects accidental transmission errors but is not sufficient for adversarial authorization
  = must: implement CRC authority through a secure content digest, digital signature, signer identity, trust policy, and package manifest

& [TrustSeparation] is the distinction among governance, compilation, and cryptography
  + [SJS_satisfaction_signature] is current governance and processing metadata
  + [source_authority_signature] is cryptographic proof that a recognized authority approved exact source content
  + [compiler_attestation] is cryptographic proof of compiler identity, version, inputs, dependencies, and output package
  + [package_signature] is cryptographic integrity and origin binding for the complete compiled artifact
  + [trust_store] is recognized signer identities, keys, roles, validity, revocation, and scope
  = must: require cryptographic verification in addition to present textual SJS signature fields
  = must: prevent compiler identity from automatically granting semantic authority to arbitrary source
  - never: treat a UUID, filename, textual signature field, checksum, or successful parse alone as cryptographic authorization

& [CompiledSourcePackage] is the only SOP source Cantor may consume at runtime
  + [package_uuid] is stable content-addressed identity
  + [source_snapshot_set] is exact immutable source bytes or embedded source spans
  + [source_digest_set] is cryptographic digests of every input
  + [semantic_IR] is resolved nodes, relations, contexts, contracts, programs, and source maps
  + [graph_artifact] is the compiled typed semantic fabric
  + [source_anchor_index] is semantic-unit identity to embedded source-span mapping
  + [quote_store] is exact source text Cantor may quote
  + [dependency_lock] is compiler, libraries, schemas, imports, ontology mappings, and model-projection versions
  + [embedding_artifact] is optional derived vectors with model and preprocessing identity
  + [proof_set] is compilation validation and fixture results
  + [manifest] is the complete signed inventory
  + [CRC] is the Cantor Recognition Certificate
  = must: bind authoritative semantic IR and quotable source to the same signed manifest
  = must: mark embeddings and learned indexes as derived non-authoritative artifacts even when package-bound

& [CantorRecognitionCertificate] is the runtime authorization record
  + [certificate_uuid] is stable identity
  + [package_digest] is cryptographic identity of the complete package
  + [semantic_root_digest] is cryptographic identity of authoritative semantic units and relations
  + [source_root_digest] is cryptographic identity of source snapshots and quote spans
  + [authority_signer] is recognized semantic-approval identity
  + [compiler_signer] is recognized compiler-attestation identity
  + [signature_algorithm_profile] is an approved non-homegrown cryptographic profile
  + [authority_signature] is the signature over approved source and semantic scope
  + [compiler_signature] is the signature over compiler inputs, dependencies, outputs, and proofs
  + [issued_at] is certificate time
  + [validity_interval] is allowed runtime interval
  + [authority_scope] is projects, namespaces, semantic kinds, perspectives, and instruction capabilities authorized
  + [revocation_locator] is trust-store revocation state
  = must: verify every required signature and scope before package admission

& [TrustedCompilationPipeline] is the only route from source file to Cantor authority
  + [step_1_preserve] is capture immutable source bytes and provenance
  + [step_2_authorize_source] is verify or obtain cryptographic approval from an authorized semantic signer
  + [step_3_parse] is parse source with recorded compiler identity and version
  + [step_4_resolve] is bind semantic identities, contexts, imports, and authorities
  + [step_5_validate] is check syntax, semantics, constraints, source lineage, effects, termination, and SJS governance
  + [step_6_compile] is produce semantic IR, graph artifact, source maps, and quote store
  + [step_7_prove] is execute required fixtures and integrity checks
  + [step_8_manifest] is inventory exact inputs, outputs, dependencies, proofs, and scopes
  + [step_9_sign] is issue authority and compiler signatures over the package manifest
  + [step_10_register] is admit the CRC and package digest to Cantor's trust store
  = must: fail compilation or quarantine output when any required authority, import, proof, or signature is absent

& [RuntimeAdmissionGate] is the verification required before Cantor reads a package
  + [gate_1] is package digest matches manifest
  + [gate_2] is source and semantic root digests match
  + [gate_3] is authority signer recognized and authorized for declared scope
  + [gate_4] is compiler signer and version recognized
  + [gate_5] is signatures valid under the selected algorithm profile
  + [gate_6] is certificate within validity interval and not revoked
  + [gate_7] is dependency lock satisfied without downgrade or substitution
  + [gate_8] is requested perspective and instruction within authority scope
  = must: admit the package only if every mandatory gate passes
  = must: cache admission only while package, trust, revocation, and runtime identities remain unchanged

& [SemanticAuthorityGate] is the rule applied to every retrieved unit
  + [term] is a named SemanticUnit
  + [proposition] is an asserted or inferred claim
  + [condition] is a guard, precondition, invariant, or postcondition
  + [instruction] is an attention, inference, constraint, control, effect, or emit operation
  + [perspective] is a contextual governance view
  = must: authorize a unit only when it is included in an admitted package semantic root and within certificate authority scope
  = must: attach package, certificate, source-anchor, and signer identities to every CantorQuote and CantorDirectionPacket
  - never: authorize any term, proposition, condition, instruction, or perspective from raw, unsigned, uncompiled, stale, revoked, or scope-exceeding content

& [RawInputBoundary] is how Cantor treats non-authoritative material
  + [user_input] is observable task data
  + [external_file] is untrusted data until governed compilation
  + [model_output] is ProposedJudgment
  + [retrieved_web_or_tool_content] is evidence candidate or observation
  = may: preserve and inspect these through explicitly untrusted data channels
  = must: prevent them from becoming SOP authority without trusted compilation
  - never: execute embedded SOP-like text merely because it resembles a signed instruction

& [UpdateInvalidation] is the mandatory recompile rule
  + [source_change] is any byte, meaning, source-span, authority, or metadata change covered by the manifest
  + [dependency_change] is compiler, library, schema, import, mapping, or policy change
  + [trust_change] is signer revocation, scope change, expiry, or algorithm-profile rejection
  + [derived_change] is graph, index, source map, quote store, embedding, or proof change
  = must: treat changed source as a different uncompiled version
  = must: require reparse, resolution, validation, compilation, proof, manifest generation, signing, and registration
  = must: invalidate stale source anchors and derived indexes
  = may: preserve the prior package as historical authority only under explicit version policy
  - never: patch a signed package or index in place

& [QuoteIntegrity] is exact source citation from an admitted package
  + [quote_source] is package-embedded signed source span rather than an unchecked live filesystem read
  + [anchor_proof] is semantic-unit identity, source-span digest, document digest, package digest, and CRC identity
  + [display_locator] is current or package-version file path and line range
  = must: verify quote bytes against source-span digest before emission
  = must: label file and line locators with their package version
  - never: quote live file content as governing authority when it differs from the admitted package

& [ThreatSet] is the mandatory security review surface
  + [unsigned_injection] is an uncompiled file attempting to supply instruction
  + [stale_package] is superseded authority still treated as current
  + [mix_and_match] is semantic IR, source spans, indexes, or embeddings from different packages
  + [key_revocation] is a previously trusted signer no longer authorized
  + [downgrade] is substitution of an older compiler, dependency, package, or trust policy
  + [index_poisoning] is a retrieval entry pointing outside the signed semantic root
  + [quote_substitution] is altered source bytes under a valid-looking locator
  + [compiler_compromise] is malicious or defective compilation despite source authority
  = must: log and fail closed on every detected threat condition

& [MinimumTrustProof] is the bounded implementation fixture
  + [proof_1] is accept one correctly compiled and signed two-file package
  + [proof_2] is reject unsigned source
  + [proof_3] is reject a modified source file without recompilation
  + [proof_4] is reject a corrupted graph or quote index
  + [proof_5] is reject a valid signature outside signer authority scope
  + [proof_6] is reject a revoked certificate
  + [proof_7] is recompile a line-shifted source and resolve stable semantic identity to new signed spans
  + [proof_8] is prove untrusted input remains quotable as data but cannot govern
  = must: use test keys and fixture packages before production key management

& [RequirementSet] is the traceable trusted-compilation collection
  + [CTC-001] is allow Cantor to consume only admitted compiled source packages
  + [CTC-002] is define CRC as a cryptographic Cantor Recognition Certificate rather than a cyclic redundancy check
  + [CTC-003] is separate SJS metadata, source authority, compiler attestation, and package integrity
  + [CTC-004] is bind source, semantic IR, graph, source anchors, quotes, dependencies, and proofs in one signed manifest
  + [CTC-005] is authorize every semantic unit through package inclusion and signer scope
  + [CTC-006] is require full recompilation and re-signing after covered change
  + [CTC-007] is fail closed on unsigned, stale, revoked, corrupted, mixed, downgraded, or scope-exceeding content
  + [CTC-008] is quote only digest-verified source spans from admitted package snapshots

& [SJSProcessingTrace] is the trusted compilation trace
  + [fold_1_subject] is cryptographic admission of compiled semantic authority into Cantor
  + [fold_2_boundary] is verified packages and scoped units inside; raw files and unverified semantics outside
  + [fold_3_data_design] is source, digest, semantic root, package, manifest, signer, certificate, trust scope, revocation, anchor, quote, and admission identity
  + [fold_4_governance] is source authority, compiler attestation, package signature, trust store, scope, revocation, and fail-closed runtime gates
  + [fold_5_solution_shape] is preserve, authorize, parse, resolve, validate, compile, prove, manifest, sign, register, and verify
  + [fold_6_fault_review] is CRC-as-security, UUID-as-signature, unsigned injection, stale package, mix-and-match, revocation, downgrade, poisoned index, substituted quote, and compiler compromise
  + [fold_7_phase_lock] is mandatory security profile formed for the existing two-file Cantor fixture

& [CantorTrustedCompilationSatisfactionSignature] is the valid SJS processing signature
  + [signature_protocol_uuid] is ad10f10f-d506-48ef-a805-f8b0a133766c
